const title = "Whirlpool Washer Not Draining? Causes & Fix";
const description = "Whirlpool washer won't drain? Usually the drain pump, a clog in the pump filter or hose, or the F9E1 long-drain code. Why it won't spin until it drains, safe checks, and when to call. Also Maytag/Amana. Same-day LA, $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why won't my Whirlpool washer drain?",
    a: "The usual causes on a Whirlpool are a failed or clogged drain pump, a clog in the pump filter or drain hose, a kinked drain hose, or a stuck lid lock (many Whirlpool washers won't drain or spin unless the lid locks). On the electronic models, Whirlpool flags a drain that takes too long as the F9E1 code — 'long drain,' meaning the water didn't clear in the expected time, which usually points at the pump or a clog rather than the control. Whirlpool builds Maytag, Amana, and many Kenmore (110-prefix) washers on the same platform, so the same parts and the same F9E1 apply across all of them. Clearing a filter or hose is homeowner-safe; the pump is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What does F9E1 mean on a Whirlpool washer?",
    a: "F9E1 is Whirlpool's 'long drain' code: the washer ran its drain step but the water didn't clear within the expected time, so it flags a drain problem and often stops before spinning. It's not usually a control-board fault — it's telling you the water isn't leaving fast enough. The common causes are a clogged pump filter, a kinked or clogged drain hose, a drain hose pushed too far down the standpipe (which siphons and confuses the sensor), or a failing drain pump. Start with the hose and filter; if those are clear, it's the pump. The same F9E1 appears on Maytag and Amana washers built on the Whirlpool platform."
  },
  {
    q: "Why won't my Whirlpool washer spin if it won't drain?",
    a: "By design. A washer won't spin on top of a tub full of water — it would sling water everywhere and can't balance — so the control drains first, then spins. If it can't drain, it never gets to the spin step, which is why 'won't drain' and 'won't spin' show up together and people think the spin is broken when the real fault is the drain. Fix the drain and the spin usually returns. That's also why our whirlpool washer not-draining and general won't-spin guidance overlap — clear the water first, and only then judge the spin."
  },
  {
    q: "Where is the drain pump filter on a Whirlpool washer?",
    a: "On Whirlpool front-load washers there's usually a small access panel at the bottom front; behind it is the drain pump filter (also called the coin trap), which catches coins, lint, and small items. Lay down towels, have a shallow pan ready — water will come out — then unscrew the filter and clear it. It's one of the most common and satisfying DIY fixes for a front-loader that won't drain. Many Whirlpool top-load washers don't have a user-accessible filter; on those, the clog is more often in the drain hose or the pump itself, which is where a tech comes in."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Whirlpool washer that won't drain myself?",
    a: "Often the first steps, yes. Check the drain hose for a kink, make sure it's not shoved more than a few inches into the standpipe, and clear the pump filter (coin trap) if your model has one — those handle a lot of no-drain cases safely. What to leave to a tech: replacing the drain pump, removing an item lodged in the pump housing, or a lid lock / control fault that stops the drain cycle from running. Always unplug the washer before reaching into the pump area, and be ready for standing water to spill when you open the filter or hose."
  }
];

