<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-drain Whirlpool is the pump, a clog, or a lid lock — and F9E1 names it.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Whirlpool washer won't drain, the causes are a short list: a failed or clogged drain pump, a clog in the pump filter (coin trap) or drain hose, a kinked or too-deeply-inserted drain hose, or a stuck lid lock that stops the cycle. On electronic models Whirlpool flags a drain that takes too long as the F9E1 "long drain" code — the water didn't clear in time, which points at the pump or a clog, not the control board. And because the washer drains before it spins, a no-drain fault also shows up as "won't spin." Whirlpool builds Maytag, Amana, and many 110-prefix Kenmore washers on this same platform, so the parts and F9E1 carry across. Clearing the hose or filter is homeowner-safe; the pump is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Won't drain, won't spin — same fault.</strong> A washer drains first, then spins. If it can't drain, it never reaches the spin step, so fix the drain and the spin usually comes back with it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 F9E1 -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · F9E1 — the long-drain code</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">F9E1 means the water didn't leave fast enough.</h2>

    <p>If your Whirlpool shows <strong>F9E1</strong> (older platforms may show it as an <strong>F21</strong>), it's the "long drain" code: the washer ran the drain step but the water hadn't cleared within the time it expects, so it flagged a drain fault and usually stopped before spinning. The key thing to understand is that F9E1 is almost never a control-board problem — it's a plumbing/pump problem the control is reporting.</p>

    <p>Work it in order: a kinked drain hose, a hose pushed more than a few inches down the standpipe (it siphons and throws off the sensor), a clogged pump filter, then the pump itself. The same code and the same order apply on the Maytag and Amana washers built on this platform — for the full Whirlpool code family see our <a href="/services/washer-repair/whirlpool-error-codes/">Whirlpool washer error codes</a> page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Whirlpool won't drain</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Kinked or misrouted drain hose (check first)</h3>
      <p>A kink behind the machine, or a hose shoved too far down the standpipe, stops or siphons the drain. The easiest thing to rule out first.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Clogged pump filter / coin trap (front-load)</h3>
      <p>Behind the bottom-front access panel on Whirlpool front-loaders. Coins, lint, and small items collect here and block the drain — a common, safe DIY clear.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Failed or blocked drain pump</h3>
      <p>If the hose and filter are clear, the pump can't move the water — worn out, or an item lodged in the impeller. A tech diagnosis and repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Stuck lid lock / door latch</h3>
      <p>Many Whirlpool washers won't run the drain or spin unless the lid locks. A failed lock reads as a no-drain, no-spin fault. Tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before it's a pump call.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Unplug the washer first,</strong> and be ready for standing water when you open the filter or hose.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the drain hose</strong> for a kink, and make sure it's inserted only a few inches into the standpipe — not shoved deep.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the pump filter (coin trap)</strong> behind the bottom-front panel on front-loaders — towels down, shallow pan ready.</li>
      <li><strong>Run a drain/spin cycle</strong> to confirm — if the water clears and it spins, you're done.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> the hose and the pump filter are safe homeowner territory. Replacing the drain pump, freeing an item lodged in the pump housing, or a lid-lock/control fault are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 SIBLINGS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Same platform: Maytag, Amana, Kenmore</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">One drain system, several badges.</h2>

    <p>Whirlpool builds Maytag, Amana, and many 110-prefix Kenmore washers on the same platform, which means the drain pump, the pump filter, and the F9E1 long-drain code all behave the same way across them. If yours is a Maytag, the diagnosis and the parts are identical — see our <a href="/services/washer-repair/maytag-not-draining/">Maytag washer not draining</a> page for the Maytag-specific version. A Kenmore washer's maker is set by its model prefix (110 = Whirlpool), so a 110 Kenmore drains exactly like this.</p>

    <p>The practical upshot: our techs carry the common Whirlpool-platform pumps and parts that fit the whole family, so a Whirlpool, Maytag, or Amana no-drain is usually a one-visit fix.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Hose and filter clear, still won't drain? It's the pump.</h2>

    <p>When the drain hose is straight, the pump filter is clean, and the Whirlpool still won't drain, the fault is the pump or the lid lock — both tech repairs. We test the pump, check for a lodged item, and confirm the lid lock, then get the drain (and the spin that follows it) working in one visit where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a> guide, the <a href="/services/washer-repair/wont-spin/">washer won't spin</a> page (often the same drain fault), and the <a href="/services/washer-repair/whirlpool-error-codes/">Whirlpool washer error codes</a> umbrella. Sibling brand: <a href="/services/washer-repair/maytag-not-draining/">Maytag not draining</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/washer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/washer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/washer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool washer not draining: common questions.</h2>

